course: everyday
lesson: 343
title: Accidents told too late
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: An accident is not a sin; hiding it can grow into one. Leo in Vancouver sat on Grandad's glasses, bent them, and put them back in the case. Grandad couldn't read his paper the next morning and had to go to the optician. Leo owned up, said sorry, and read the paper aloud to Grandad every morning until the new glasses came. Making amends can be a kind thing you do, not only money.
explain: Leo let the bath overflow while he played a game, mopped it with towels and hid them. Nobody noticed — until a wet patch spread across the ceiling downstairs. Dad had to fix the ceiling. Told straight away, the water would have gone no further. Now Leo sets a timer when the bath runs.
explain: Some things stay hidden for months. Leo broke the garden gnome with his bat and buried the pieces with his friend, laughing. He got away with it all winter and nearly forgot — then Dad dug the pieces up planting tomatoes in spring. Dad laughed at the gnome, but not at the hiding.
explain: The goldfish is the sad one: Leo didn't clean the water, the fish got ill and died. He told Mum the truth, they buried it together, and he looks after the new fish every week. A mess, a spill or a sick pet is easiest to fix when it is fresh — so tell at once.
fact: Most accidents are easy to forgive; hiding them is what causes trouble.
fact: A spill or stain is much easier to clean while it is fresh than after it has dried.
fact: Water from an overflowing bath can soak through the floor and damage the ceiling below.
fact: A fish needs clean water; dirty water can make it ill and kill it.
fact: Looking after a pet is a daily job you cannot skip.
fact: A broken toilet handle or leak should be told at once so it can be fixed before it gets worse; hidden, the next person can't flush and a plumber must be called.
fact: Swapping a broken thing for yours without saying so is a trick, not a fix.
fact: Making amends can be time and kindness, like reading to someone whose glasses you broke.
fact: Things that are hidden usually turn up in the end, sometimes months later.
fact: Knocking down someone's work when you are cross is unkind, even if it was quick.
exercise: Why did Leo put Grandad's bent glasses back in the case? | He was scared of being told off | He thought they would bend back by themselves | Grandad asked him to sit on them | He didn't notice they were bent at all
exercise: How did Leo make amends for the glasses? | He said sorry and read the paper to Grandad until new ones came | He bought Grandad a newspaper with his pocket money and said nothing | He hid Grandad's paper too | He said nothing and waited
exercise: Why did the goldfish get ill? | Its water wasn't cleaned | It ate too little fish food | Goldfish always get ill in winter | It was lonely in the bowl
exercise: A girl spills paint on the carpet and moves the rug over it. Why does that make it worse? | The paint dries and the stain can't come out | Rugs make paint spread across the whole floor | Carpets clean themselves | Paint fades under rugs
exercise: A boy breaks a classmate's ruler and swaps it for his own. The classmate sees the name. What does he call it? | Sneaky, because the swap was a trick | Fair, since all rulers are the same anyway | Kind, because he got a ruler back anyway | Funny, because it was only a little joke
exercise: How did Leo's buried gnome get found? | Dad dug up the pieces planting tomatoes | His friend told on him at once to Dad and Mum | The gnome was on the news | A dog dug it up that night
exercise: Leo got away with the gnome all winter. What does that show? | Hidden things usually turn up in the end | He was clever to bury it so deep in the soil | Winter makes things disappear | Dad didn't care about gnomes
exercise: A boy knocks over the little ones' tower because they wouldn't let him play. What is the fair way to put it right? | Say sorry and help them build it again | Tell them they should've let him play in the first place | Say it fell by itself | Build his own taller tower
exercise: A boy breaks the toilet flush handle and says nothing. What happens? | The next person can't flush, and a plumber must be called | It fixes itself overnight when nobody is using it, as most toilets do | Nobody uses the toilet | Nothing happens at all
exercise: Why is it hard to tell about something embarrassing, like a broken toilet handle? | You feel silly, but it is still better told | Embarrassing things never need telling to anyone | Toilets don't matter | Grown-ups never want to know
exercise: Leo let the bath overflow and hid the towels. Where did the trouble show up? | As a wet patch on the ceiling below | Nowhere, the towels soak it all up | In the garden | In his bedroom only
exercise: What did Leo and Dad do so the bath wouldn't overflow again? | Set a timer whenever the bath runs | Put more towels on the floor, just in case | Stopped using the bath and had showers | Took the plug out and threw it away for good
exercise: When is a spill or a mess easiest to fix? | When it is fresh and told at once | After it dries and goes hard, so it peels off | Next week | When someone else finds it
exercise: What is the difference between an accident and hiding it? | An accident just happens; hiding it is a choice | They are the same thing, really, both just happen | Hiding is the accident | Accidents are always on purpose
exercise: What did Leo do after the goldfish died? | Told Mum the truth and now cleans the new fish's water weekly | Bought a new one and hoped Mum wouldn't notice the different colour | Said the fish was old | Flushed it and said nothing
test: Grandad, squinting: «Who's been sitting on my specs?» What's the kind and honest answer? | «Me, by accident. Sorry, I'll read you the paper» | «You sat on them yourself, Grandad, I saw you do it» | «Maybe the dog» | «They were like that»
test: Mum, lifting the rug: «WHAT is this? When did this happen??» What should the girl say? | The truth, and offer to help scrub it | «It was already there when I came in» | «The paint fell from the ceiling» | «The dog did it»
test: Bro: «just bin the bits, Dad'll never notice the gnome's gone». What is wiser? | Tell Dad and say sorry; hidden bits turn up | Buy a gnome secretly and swap it in the garden | Bury them deeper | Blame the fox
test: Dad downstairs: «Why is there a WET PATCH on the ceiling?!» What helps most now? | Tell him about the bath straight away | Say it's raining inside the house again | Hide more towels | Say it was always there
test: Kid: «I've never told about the stuff I break and I've never been caught». What's true? | Things usually turn up, and hiding makes them worse | Hiding always works for small things if you're careful | He'll never be caught | It's fine if he's careful
test: Little kid at after-school club, crying: «Who smashed our tower?» The boy did, cross. What should he do? | Own up, say sorry and help rebuild it | Say a big kid did it and ran off outside | Say towers are boring | Walk away
test: Mum: «The fish is floating on its side. Have you been changing the water?» What's the honest answer? | «No, I didn't. I'm sorry» | «Every day, I promise» | «It just got old» | «Fish do that»
test: Classmate: «That's not my ruler, that's got YOUR name on it». What is the fix? | Tell him the truth and give him a new ruler | Say the names got swapped by magic in the night | Keep both rulers | Say he's mistaken
test: Dad, plunger in hand: «Who broke the flush?» The boy did and is embarrassed. What should he do? | Own up; embarrassing things are still better told | Say it was the plumber who broke it last time | Pretend to be asleep | Say it broke by itself
test: Which is TRUE about telling about an accident at once? | It often stops it getting worse and is easier to forgive | It always gets you a bigger punishment than hiding it would | It never makes a difference | It is only for big accidents
test: «Did u tell anyone u broke Grandad's glasses?» «Nope, put em back in the case». What will most likely happen? | Grandad finds them bent and needs new ones anyway | Glasses heal in the case | Grandad never wears them | Nothing happens
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the concealment of an overflow of bath water. | Water may penetrate the floor and damage the ceiling of the room beneath | Concealed water evaporates overnight without consequence of any kind | Towels invariably absorb the entire quantity | Overflows occasion no damage whatsoever
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a spillage ought to be reported without delay. | It may be remedied more readily whilst fresh | It may thereby be concealed far more effectively | It ceases to be a spillage after a short interval | Delay has no bearing whatsoever upon the remedy
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises reparation for the accidental breaking of the spectacles of a grandparent? | It may consist in acts of service, such as reading aloud, as well as in money | It may consist solely in the payment of money | It is unnecessary where the breakage was accidental | It is required only where the grandparent so demands
exam: Ascertain the most probable outcome of the burial of the fragments of a broken garden ornament. | The fragments are commonly discovered at a later date | The fragments are never discovered once they are buried in soil | The ornament is thereby restored | The owner is thereby compensated
